  "body": "Having element hyperlink to a hidden element doesn't make any sense because the hyperlink would simply be broken. However, referring to a hidden element via `for` attribute is different.\r\n\r\nAs mentioned in the first paragraph of `hidden` attribute:\r\n> When specified on an element, it indicates that the element is ... being used to declare content to be reused by other parts of the page as opposed to being directly accessed by the user.\r\n\r\nAuthors may use `hidden` attribute to hide an element, but show its state elsewhere in the page, and use an element with `for` attribute referring to a `hidden` element to control the state of the element.\r\n\r\nIt could be especially useful to combine `label` element with checkbox, e.g.\r\n```html\r\n<style>\r\n#a + label {\r\n  color: black;\r\n}\r\n#a:checked + label {\r\n  color: green;\r\n  font-weight: bold;\r\n}\r\n</style>\r\n<input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"a\" hidden><label for=\"a\">check this</label>\r\n```\r\nthen the `label` can be used to both control and reflect the checkbox state.\r\n\r\nI think this is a valid usecase, so the spec shouldn't use \"must not\" to forbid it.",
